WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.900
Professor Dave here, let’s prove the Pythagorean
theorem.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.760
As I mentioned before when we initially learned
the Pythagorean theorem, there are very elegant

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:21.471
ways to prove that the theorem is true, and
now that we know how to calculate area, we

00:00:21.471 --> 00:00:25.310
are ready to perform one such proof ourselves.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:31.819
Let’s say we draw a right triangle like
this one, and label the sides A, B, and C,

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:33.590
like we are used to.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.190
Now let’s make three more copies of this
triangle and arrange them like this, so that

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.420
we get two squares.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.140
The larger square has a side length of A plus
B, while the smaller inner square has a side

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.600
length of C.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.120
We can calculate the areas of all of these
shapes.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.239
We know that the area of one of the triangles
will be one half base times height, or one

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:06.000
half A times B. The area of the large square
will be the side length squared, or A plus

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.149
B quantity squared.

00:01:08.149 --> 00:01:12.430
And the small square will have an area of
C squared.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:16.250
Now let’s set up a relationship between
these areas.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:22.320
The area of the large square is equal to A
plus B quantity squared as we just said, but

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.490
it is also equal to the area of the small
square plus the area of all the triangles,

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.520
since these shapes comprise the larger square.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.670
So we can make an equation that states the
following.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:42.210
A plus B quantity squared, the area of the
big square, is equal to four times one half

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:49.659
AB, which is four times the area of one triangle,
since there are four triangles, plus C squared,

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:52.250
the area of the small square.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.109
We know how to square a binomial by using
the FOIL method, so let’s take away the

00:01:57.109 --> 00:02:00.930
exponent and just write the binomial out twice.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:10.750
First gives us A squared, outer gives us AB,
inner gives us AB, and last gives us B squared.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:17.450
Combining like terms, that’s A squared plus
two AB plus B squared.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:24.840
On the right, we can multiply four and one
half, and we get two AB plus C squared.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:31.331
As it happens, we have two AB on both sides,
so those will cancel out, and wouldn’t you

00:02:31.331 --> 00:02:34.660
know it, take a look at what we are left with.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.970
The Pythagorean theorem.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:44.310
I hope this result makes you feel warm and
fuzzy inside, because proofs like this are

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.750
what math is really all about.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:52.370
There are many other proofs of this theorem
that involve similar calculations with triangles

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:57.380
and squares and other shapes, so if you’re
just dying to know them all, it’s time to

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.380
hit the internet.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.400
Otherwise, let’s move forward and wrap things
up with geometry.

